.TH SEARCH 1
.SH NAME
Search \- find anything by part of it, in acme
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B Search
[
.B -s
.I letters
] [
.I dir
|
.I query ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Search
opens an
.IR acme (1)
window,
.IB dir /+Search\f1.
Its first line is a query, and the lines after it are what matches it, best first, as many as a
hundred and at most five from one file, changing as the query is typed.
A result is a file, or a file and a line,
.IB file : line
followed by the line's text.
.PP
The results come from sources, the lines of
.B /lib/acme/search
or
.B $home/lib/acme/search
in its place: one a line, a letter, flags, and an
.IR rc (1)
command.
The flags are a character each, or
.B -
for none.
With
.BR s ,
the source is static: it runs once, and its lines are filtered as the query is typed.
Otherwise it runs again at each pause in typing, stopping the run before it.
A command runs by
.B rc -c
in
.IR root ,
with
.I root
in
.B $root
and the query's pattern in
.BR $pattern ,
so a quote or a semicolon in the pattern stays part of the word:
.EX
	t	-	grep -n $pattern `{walk -f .} /dev/null
.EE
.PP
The sources see
.I root
through
.I srvfs
(see
.IR exportfs (4))
with a pattern file that hides every name starting with a dot, read only, so
.B .git
and other hidden files stay out of the results.
The sources are
.B w
(the windows of acme),
.B f
(files),
.B g
(the lines of files, by
.IR grep (1)),
.B s
(the symbols the language servers of
.IR lspfs (4)
running for
.I root
give for the query) and
.B d
(the diagnostics they have for files under
.IR root ).
.B -s
names the sources searched; with none, all.
A query ending
.BI + letters
searches those alone.
.PP
A query is words separated by blanks, and a result must match each:
a word's letters in order, counting for more at the start of a name or a word, in a run, and in
a file's own name;
.BI ' word
the word itself;
.BI ^ word
at the start;
.IB word $
at the end; and
.BI ! word
not anywhere.
A word with no upper-case letter ignores case.
A query source is given the words to look for, without their marks, as its pattern.
.PP
A newline typed at the end of the query opens the first result;
button 3 or button 2 on a result opens that one, by the plumber's
.B edit
port, at its line.
The window stays, with its query, until
.BR Del .
Given text that is not a directory, as when
.I Search
is run with a chorded argument,
.I Search
starts with that text as its query.
.PP
The files are those of
.I dir
and below, as
.IR walk (1)
gives them; in a git repository,
.I dir
is the repository's top.
With no
.IR dir ,
.I Search
takes the directory of the file in acme window
.B $acmewin
or
.BR $winid ,
or else the current directory.
.SH EXAMPLES
.I Acmestart
binds Command-P and Command-Shift-F in
.BR /lib/hotkey/acme :
.IP
.EX
cmd-p	{test -e /mnt/acme/index || mount $acmesrv /mnt/acme; Search -s wf}
cmd-F	{test -e /mnt/acme/index || mount $acmesrv /mnt/acme; Search -s gs}
.EE
.PP
Find a function's callers by grep alone, but not its tests:
.IP
.EX
decodeToken !_test+g
.EE
.SH FILES
.TF /lib/acme/search
.TP
.B /lib/acme/search
the sources
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/Search.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR acme (1),
.IR grep (1),
.IR hotkey (1),
.IR lsp (1),
.IR lspfs (4),
.IR plumb (1),
.IR walk (1)
